## Solution

Let $X$ be a real Hilbert space, $A\in\mathscr{L}(X)$ a bounded linear operator, and $F(x)=\langle x,Ax\rangle$. We compute $d^{n}F$ for $n\ge 1$.

### First derivative

For $x,h\in X$ and $t\in\mathbb{R}$,
$$
F(x+th)=\langle x+th,\,A(x+th)\rangle=\langle x,Ax\rangle+t\big(\langle x,Ah\rangle+\langle h,Ax\rangle\big)+t^{2}\langle h,Ah\rangle.
$$
Hence
$$
dF(x)[h]=\langle x,Ah\rangle+\langle h,Ax\rangle.
$$
Using the adjoint $A^{*}$ (defined by $\langle u,Av\rangle=\langle A^{*}u,v\rangle$) and symmetry of the real inner product,
$$
\langle x,Ah\rangle=\langle A^{*}x,h\rangle,\qquad \langle h,Ax\rangle=\langle Ax,h\rangle,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\,dF(x)[h]=\big\langle (A+A^{*})x,\;h\big\rangle.\,}
$$

### Second derivative

View $dF:X\to X^{*}$ as the map $x\mapsto \big(h\mapsto \langle (A+A^{*})x,h\rangle\big)$. For directions $h,k\in X$,
$$
d^{2}F(x)[h,k]=\lim_{t\to 0}\frac{dF(x+th)[k]-dF(x)[k]}{t}.
$$
Since
$$
dF(x+th)[k]=\big\langle (A+A^{*})(x+th),\,k\big\rangle=\big\langle (A+A^{*})x,k\big\rangle+t\,\big\langle (A+A^{*})h,k\big\rangle,
$$
we obtain
$$
\boxed{\,d^{2}F(x)[h,k]=\big\langle (A+A^{*})h,\;k\big\rangle.\,}
$$
This is independent of $x$; thus $d^{2}F$ is a **constant** bilinear form on $X$.

### Higher derivatives ($n\ge 3$)

Since $d^{2}F$ is constant (its value does not depend on $x$), every further directional derivative vanishes:
$$
d^{3}F(x)[h,k,\ell]=\lim_{t\to 0}\frac{d^{2}F(x+t\ell)[h,k]-d^{2}F(x)[h,k]}{t}=0,
$$
and inductively
$$
\boxed{\,d^{n}F\equiv 0\quad\text{for all }n\ge 3.\,}
$$

### Summary

For $n\ge 1$,
$$
d^{n}F(x)=
\begin{cases}
\big[h\mapsto \langle (A+A^{*})x,\,h\rangle\big], & n=1,\\[4pt]
\big[(h,k)\mapsto \langle (A+A^{*})h,\,k\rangle\big], & n=2,\\[4pt]
0, & n\ge 3.
\end{cases}
$$

Equivalently, $d^{2}F(x)[h,k]=\langle (A+A^{*})h,k\rangle$ is the constant symmetric bilinear form associated with the self-adjoint part $A+A^{*}$, and $F$ is a polynomial of degree $2$ in $x$, so all derivatives of order $\ge 3$ vanish identically.

